  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Thanaram And Anr vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:2132) on 10 January, 2024",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner had undergone detention for some period and the case is pending against him since 1995. Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner is facing agony of a long protracted trial and therefore, without making any interference on merits/conviction, the sentences awarded to the present revisionist/petitioner may be substituted with the period of sentences already undergone by him. Learned Public Prosecutor opposes the submissions made on behalf of the petitioner. However, he was not in a position to dispute that the present revision petition is pending since 2004. Hon'ble the Supreme Court of India in the case of Alister Anthony Pareira Vs. State of Maharashtra (2012)2 SCC 648 and Haripada Das Vs. State of W.B. (1998)9 SCC 678, pleased to observe as under: Alister Anthony Pareira (supra) [2024:RJ-JD:2132] (3 of 3) [CRLR-506/2004] \"There is no straitjacket formula for sentencing an accused on proof of crime. The courts have evolved certain principles: twin objective of the sentencing policy is deterrence and correction.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner had undergone detention for some period and the case is pending against him since 1995. Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner is facing agony of a long protracted trial and therefore, without making any interference on merits/conviction, the sentences awarded to the present revisionist/petitioner may be substituted with the period of sentences already undergone by him. Learned Public Prosecutor opposes the submissions made on behalf of the petitioner. However, he was not in a position to dispute that the present revision petition is pending since 2004.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Hon'ble the Supreme Court of India in the case of Alister Anthony Pareira Vs. State of Maharashtra (2012)2 SCC 648 and Haripada Das Vs. State of W.B. (1998)9 SCC 678, pleased to observe as under: Alister Anthony Pareira (supra) [2024:RJ-JD:2132] (3 of 3) [CRLR-506/2004] \"There is no straitjacket formula for sentencing an accused on proof of crime. The courts have evolved certain principles: twin objective of the sentencing policy is deterrence and correction."
  },
